  "narrative_body": "Hungary summoned Russia's ambassador and demanded an explanation for drone strikes on Transcarpathia, the region of western Ukraine where Hungary's ethnic minority lives — the sharpest break yet with the Orbán era's deference to Moscow.\n\nAnita Orbán, the foreign minister, told Ambassador Evgeny Stanislavov the attacks were \"completely unacceptable\" and asked publicly when Russia plans to end \"this bloody war.\" The ambassador left within 30 minutes. Péter Magyar, the prime minister, personally condemned the strikes. President Tamás Sulyok condemned them independently. The Transcarpathia framing is politically crafted as well as diplomatically significant: routing the condemnation through Hungary's ethnic minority in Ukraine simultaneously breaks with Moscow and activates the Trianon solidarity narrative — a cross-partisan emotional substrate that makes the pivot durable at home. On the same day the full cabinet was sworn in, Hungary also lifted its veto blocking new EU sanctions against Israeli settlers, a move that cost nothing domestically and restored goodwill in Brussels. Later in the week, Mr Magyar announced the end of both the six-year COVID emergency and the four-year Ukraine war emergency, dismantling the decree-rule structures most often cited as instruments of Orbán-era democratic backsliding. Three alignment actions in five days, each individually low-risk, together confirm a behavioural pattern now documented across four consecutive weeks.\n\nThe domestic agenda was equally kinetic, though its architecture differs from its optics. Mr Magyar opened the Karmelita Monastery — Orbán's fortress-like former office — to public tours, dismantled its security fencing, and led journalists through what he called its luxury renovations. He released footage of 15 to 20 sacks of shredded documents alongside Fidesz campaign materials found in the basement of the former transport ministry, announcing a criminal complaint for alleged illegal party financing using state resources. He published renovation photos of the Kúria, Hungary's supreme court, alleging a bar, gilded ceilings, and a presidential suite, and demanded Chief Justice Varga Zs. András resign. He also fired Bertalan Havasi, Orbán's longtime press secretary, via a Saturday gazette notice; Fidesz politicians challenged the legality of the move, an independent fact-check confirmed the firing was valid, and the challenge posts were quietly deleted.\n\nAcross all of these actions the pattern is the same: public exposure of alleged misconduct as pressure for voluntary resignation, rather than formal legal removal. Sulyok — whom 60% of Hungarians in polls want gone — has refused to resign. So has Varga Zs. András. Péter Polt, the prosecutor general, has said nothing. The constitutional mechanisms to remove all three require supermajority votes, court proceedings, or legal actions that Mr Magyar's government has not yet initiated. What has been conspicuously absent in this first full week is legislation: no draft bill for accession to the European Public Prosecutor's Office has been submitted to Parliament, no public media restructuring has been announced, and no formal constitutional proceedings against any institutional head have begun. The critical question that loomed before the government took office remains open: is the communications blitz building toward architectural reform, or substituting for it?\n\nOne crack appeared in the state media without any government instruction. MTI, the state news agency, published official photos from which Sulyok had been cropped — after Mr Magyar orchestrated ministers to physically exclude the president from camera lines at the swearing-in ceremony. The head of Duna Médiaszolgáltató, the public broadcaster, ordered an internal investigation. Nobody told MTI to crop the president; editorial staff appear to have anticipated the new government's preferences and acted on their own. The informal realignment has begun before the formal kind.\n\nThe economy meanwhile delivered an early reminder of inherited constraints. The Hungarian National Bank, whose governor Mihály Varga serves until 2031 and cannot be removed by the new government, cut the implicit rate on its FX-swap facility from 5.75% to 5.25%, reversing the forint's 10% post-election rally and weakening the currency by 0.7% within hours. Finance Minister András Miklós Kármán had told Parliament that week that the government wanted cheaper borrowing costs, not a weak currency. The central bank moved in the opposite direction anyway. Analysts at MBH Bank now forecast a formal rate-cutting cycle starting in June. The constraint long identified as a structural risk has now operated in real time and in basis points.\n\nIn his first interview since leaving office, Viktor Orbán told Magyar Nemzet that the past 16 years were Hungary's \"most successful period of the past hundred years.\" He acknowledged that only 19% of voters under 40 chose Fidesz — identifying the generational collapse as the decisive defeat — and outlined a strategy of rebuilding \"from the radical fringe inward,\" starting with rural communities and working toward Budapest. A Fidesz congress is set for June 13. The interview confirmed what the structural picture predicted: Orbán intends to run an organised opposition, not retire, and will frame every institutional pressure action by Mr Magyar's government as unconstitutional persecution.",
